+## Explicit uncapped Codex mode (2026-09-19)
+
+The existing task script now has a second explicitly authorized run mode; it does
+not discover a client, credential or unbounded business-input queue. See
+[the uncapped contract](research-model-budget.md#explicit-uncapped-codex-operation-wp-02--wp-03-2026-09-19)
+for the pinned profile, isolation requirements, non-hard output bound and migration68.
+
+`create-uncapped --authorization-id ID --input-sha256 SHA256 --allow-authorization-write`
+reads one reviewed canonical authorization from binary stdin (optional one LF/CRLF),
+checks its exact hash/ID, and calls the existing managed session. No model runs.
+`inspect-uncapped --authorization-id ID` reads its immutable policy/usage metadata;
+unknown fees/provider request counts are null and unknown/failed invocations remain
+visible. Missing authorization is not an empty project. Output is published only
+after managed cleanup; short write or interruption remains nonzero.
+
+The standalone script can run a turn only with an explicit authorization and
+six-field nonsecret profile, on an explicitly prepared/approved local installation:
+
+```text
+python scripts/manage_research_tasks.py --root <approved-project-root> run-turn --rotation-id <original-rotation-id> --turn-id <explicit-new-turn-id> --batch-id <approved-batch-id> --authorization-id <stored-authorization-id> --codex-profile <explicit-profile-path> --max-tasks 1 --max-workers 1 --allow-model-calls
+```
+
+Placeholders must be supplied from operator-reviewed configuration, not copied as
+literal commands. For Python callers, `run_research_batch` and
+`run_research_rotation` accept `uncapped_authorization_id` and `codex_profile` with
+`model_factory=None`. Capped mode retains `model_budget_id` plus the approved
+application factory; the two modes are mutually exclusive. A missing run opt-in
+causes rejection before even reading the selected profile. No factory/profile,
+budget/authorization mismatch or missing credentials trigger another client.
+
+Original batch/turn hashes and claim identities are unchanged. A replayed turn
+returns its original selection receipt, not proof that a new policy was used;
+new top-level authorization fields identify the current invocation's requested
+mode only. The per-request database invocation ledger, not turn metadata, is the
+source for actual authorized work. Stop, claim-before-client, captured failure,
+unknown-commit and incomplete semantics are unchanged; no old claim is reclaimed.
+Ordinary failed output does not undo already recorded work or refund a permit.
+
+No automatic install/login, real research activation, user migration, service
+registration, alternate provider, real order or Release is performed by these
+commands without the respective explicit runtime inputs/permissions. This source
+stage is first verified on synthetic servers and private CI databases.
